The abstract describes a study evaluating the need for local anesthesia during magnetic resonance arthrography (MRa) of the shoulder. The study found that patients who received local anesthesia (group A) had a mean pain level of 2.6 ± 2.3 on a VAS, while those who did not receive local anesthesia (groups B and C) had mean pain levels of 2.6 ± 2.2 and 2.7 ± 2.4, respectively. There was no statistically significant difference in pain intensity between the three groups (p = 0.960). Additionally, there was a significant difference in pain perception between men and women (p = 0.009), but the sex difference was equal across all three groups (p = 0.934).

While the study shows that local anesthesia was associated with slightly lower pain levels, the lack of a statistically significant difference between groups suggests that the need for local anesthesia may not be definitively established. The sample size is adequate, but the results are not conclusive enough to definitively answer whether local anesthesia is necessary. Therefore, the answer is "maybe."

